  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A museum sells daily entry passes. The price of each pass is $$20.$ The cost of operating the museum includes a fixed daily cost of $$1,100$ and a variable cost of $$5$ per pass sold. If the museum can sell at most $300$ passes per day, what is the number of passes that maximizes the museum's profit?\n\nThe number of passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ passes per day, the museum has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=20x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the museum is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{1100}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{5x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =20x-(1100+5x) \\\\  & =15x-1100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 300.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=300$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=15,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=300.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1100, \\\\ P(300) & =15(300)-1100=4500-1100=3400\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of passes that maximizes the museum’s profit is $300.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
